\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Transport of polynomial observables and functionals}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring. For a #{k}-module #{M},
write #{\mathcal P_k(M)} for the unital #{k}-subalgebra of functions
#{M\to k} generated by ordinary linear functionals. This extends the
[intrinsic real polynomial functions](fdip-002V) using the same
pointwise operations. These are actual functions: over finite fields,
distinct formal polynomials can have identical evaluations.}

\p{For a linear isomorphism #{P:M\to M'}, pullback gives a unital
algebra isomorphism
##{P^*:\mathcal P_k(M')\longrightarrow\mathcal P_k(M),\qquad
   P^*F'=F'\circ P.}
An already supplied [normalized functional](fdip-0021) #{\omega} on
#{\mathcal P_k(M)} transports to #{\omega'=\omega\circ P^*}.
For the [moving split square](fdip-003E), the retained functionals
#{\omega_X=\omega\circ\pi^*} and
#{\omega'_{X'}=\omega'\circ(\pi')^*} satisfy
##{\omega'_{X'}=\omega_X\circ P_X^*.}
Here #{\pi:M\to X}, #{\pi':M'\to X'} are the specified
projections, and #{\pi'P=P_X\pi}.}

\proof{
\p{Precomposition preserves sums, scalar multiples, products and the
constant function #{1}, by evaluation. A linear functional on #{M'}
composed with #{P} is linear on #{M}, so pullback preserves the
generated polynomial algebras. Its inverse is #{(P^{-1})^*}.
For composable maps,
#{(P'P)^*F''=F''\circ P'\circ P=P^*((P')^*F'')}; identity
pullback is identity. This proves contravariance as well.}

\p{Linearity of #{\omega'} and #{\omega'(1)=\omega(1)=1}
follow directly, or by [algebra-map pullback](fdip-0027).
The section #{\iota_X} of #{\pi} makes #{\pi^*} injective:
#{f\circ\pi=g\circ\pi} implies #{f=g} after composition with
#{\iota_X}. Thus #{\omega_X} is restriction to the image of
#{\pi^*}, identified with #{\mathcal P_k(X)}. Finally, for each
#{f'\in\mathcal P_k(X')} and #{x\in M},
##{\begin{aligned}
P^*(\pi')^*f'(x)
 &=f'(\pi'Px)\\
 &=f'(P_X\pi x)=(\pi^*P_X^*f')(x).
\end{aligned}}
Applying #{\omega} gives the asserted retained-functional equality.}
}

\p{Over #{\mathbb R} with the identity involution, or over
#{\mathbb C} with pointwise conjugation, suppose the chosen observable
algebras are closed under that involution. Pullback then commutes with
star. If #{\omega} is an [algebraic state](fdip-0024),
##{\begin{aligned}
\omega'(F'^*F')
 &=\omega\bigl((P^*F')^*(P^*F')\bigr)\geq0,
\end{aligned}}
and the star compatibility of the functional is preserved too.
Holomorphic polynomial functions on a complex vector space are not
automatically closed under conjugation; closure is an extra hypothesis.
One can instead generate an algebra by both linear functionals and
their conjugates; precomposition preserves those generators as well.
No positivity over an arbitrary ring or existence of a measure is
asserted. For the real Gaussian, [absolute moments](fdip-002W) first
give its [polynomial state](fdip-002X); the
[transported law](fdip-000H) then identifies this algebraic transport
with expectation. Integrable functions as a whole are not an algebra,
and restriction here is neither conditional expectation nor partial trace.}

\p{The algebra and state conventions are
\citet{Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks} and
\citet{Definitions 3.7 and 3.9, p. 11}{waldmann2004states}.
The composition and split-square calculations are direct extensions
of \ref{fdip-002A} and \ref{fdip-0027}; their linear-dual and
star-algebra interfaces do not supply a Gaussian measure.}
